    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.grammar_units, $seed${"slug":"b2-non-defining-relative-clauses","title":"Non-defining relative clauses: adding extra information","level":"B2","status":"published","sort_order":200,"metadata":{},"focus_area":"relative-clauses","explanation_text":"A non-defining relative clause adds extra information about a noun that is already clear. It is separated by commas: My sister, who lives in Seoul, is a nurse. Use who for people, which for things, whose for possession, where for places and when for times. In a non-defining clause you cannot use that, and you cannot leave the relative pronoun out. which can also refer back to a whole idea: He passed the exam, which surprised everyone. A defining clause has no commas, because it tells us which person or thing we mean: The man who called you is my uncle.","contrast_text":"Compare: My brother who works in IT lives in Tashkent (I have more than one brother; this tells you which one). My brother, who works in IT, lives in Tashkent (I have one brother; his job is extra information).","common_error_pattern":"Using that after a comma, joining two sentences with only a comma ('..., it was...'), or leaving out the commas around extra information.","examples_json":[{"bad":"Samarkand, that is over 2,500 years old, attracts many tourists.","good":"Samarkand, which is over 2,500 years old, attracts many tourists."},{"bad":"The train was late, what meant we missed the meeting.","good":"The train was late, which meant we missed the meeting."},{"bad":"Dilnoza, her brother is my classmate, is coming tonight.","good":"Dilnoza, whose brother is my classmate, is coming tonight."},{"bad":"We spent a day in Bukhara, there we saw the Ark fortress.","good":"We spent a day in Bukhara, where we saw the Ark fortress."}],"tags":["relative clauses","non-defining","commas"]}$seed$::jsonb) r
